    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Human capital</span> refers to the knowledge, skills, health, and abilities embodied in a population, which enable people to work productively and contribute to economic growth.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Definition and Characteristics of Human Capital</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Human capital is developed through education, training, and healthcare, and unlike physical capital, it cannot be physically separated from the person who possesses it. Key characteristics include: it appreciates with continued investment (learning and experience), it can migrate (move with the person), and it deteriorates without maintenance (e.g., skills becoming outdated).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Human Capital vs. Physical Capital</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Human Capital</th><th>Physical Capital</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Embodied in people (skills, knowledge, health)</td><td>Consists of tangible assets (machines, buildings, tools)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cannot be bought or sold directly; developed through education/training</td><td>Can be directly bought, sold, or transferred as property</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Can migrate with the person</td><td>Fixed or transported as physical goods</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Depreciates through skill obsolescence or poor health</td><td>Depreciates through physical wear and tear</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Factors Affecting the Efficiency of Human Capital</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Quality and relevance of education and training received</li>\n  <li>Health status of the workforce, affecting productivity</li>\n  <li>Availability of tools, technology, and a supportive work environment</li>\n  <li>Motivation and working conditions, including wages</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Brain Drain and Its Effect on the Nigerian Economy</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Brain drain</span> refers to the emigration of highly skilled and educated individuals (such as doctors, engineers, and academics) from a country, usually to seek better economic opportunities abroad. For Nigeria, brain drain results in a loss of skilled professionals, reduced quality of essential services (like healthcare), lost investment in the education of those who leave, and a slower pace of national development.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Strategies for Arresting Brain Drain</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Improving wages and working conditions to be competitive with international standards</li>\n  <li>Investing in modern infrastructure and equipment for professionals to work with</li>\n  <li>Creating a stable, secure environment with good governance</li>\n  <li>Offering incentives, such as tax breaks or research grants, to retain and attract skilled professionals</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: human capital \"migrates\" with the individual, which is exactly why brain drain is possible — a skilled worker can simply relocate, taking their human capital with them, unlike a factory's physical capital.\n</div>\n",
