    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Petroleum</span> has been central to the Nigerian economy since its discovery in commercial quantities in 1956, shaping government revenue, foreign exchange earnings, and national development in profound ways — both positively and negatively.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Development of the Petroleum Industry in Nigeria</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Crude oil was discovered in commercial quantities at Oloibiri in 1956, with exports beginning in 1958. Following the Nigerian Civil War, oil became increasingly central to the economy, especially after the 1970s oil price boom, which dramatically increased government revenue.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Positive Contributions of Petroleum</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Major source of government revenue through taxes and royalties</li>\n  <li>Primary source of foreign exchange earnings for Nigeria</li>\n  <li>Funding for infrastructure and development projects</li>\n  <li>Employment generation in the oil and related sectors</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Negative Contributions (\"Resource Curse\" Effects)</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Neglect of other sectors:</strong> Over-reliance on oil has led to reduced investment in agriculture and manufacturing (the \"Dutch Disease\" effect)</li>\n  <li><strong>Environmental degradation:</strong> Oil spills and gas flaring have damaged ecosystems, particularly in the Niger Delta</li>\n  <li><strong>Revenue volatility:</strong> Government income fluctuates heavily with unpredictable global oil prices</li>\n  <li><strong>Corruption and mismanagement:</strong> Oil wealth has, at times, been linked to increased corruption</li>\n  <li><strong>Regional conflict:</strong> Disputes over oil revenue distribution have fuelled tension in oil-producing regions</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Roles of NNPC and OPEC</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Organisation</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>NNPC (Nigerian National Petroleum Corporation)</td><td>Nigeria's state oil corporation, involved in exploration, production, refining, marketing, and distribution of petroleum products domestically</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>OPEC (Organization of the Petroleum Exporting Countries)</td><td>An international cartel of oil-exporting nations (including Nigeria) that coordinates production levels among members to help stabilise global oil prices</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> When discussing petroleum's role in Nigeria, always present a balanced view — mention both the positive revenue/foreign exchange contributions AND the negative \"resource curse\" effects, as WAEC often asks for both sides.\n</div>\n",
