    "topicCode": "SS3-ECO-08",
    "module": "International Economics",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">International trade</span> is the exchange of goods and services across national borders, allowing countries to access resources and products they cannot efficiently produce themselves.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of International Trade</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">International trade is the buying and selling of goods and services between residents of different countries, involving exports (goods sold to other countries) and imports (goods bought from other countries).</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Domestic Trade vs. International Trade</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Domestic Trade</th><th>International Trade</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Occurs within one country's borders</td><td>Occurs between two or more different countries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uses a single currency</td><td>Often involves currency exchange between different national currencies</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Subject to a single set of national laws and regulations</td><td>Subject to multiple countries' laws, tariffs, and trade agreements</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Generally fewer restrictions on movement of goods</td><td>May face tariffs, quotas, and customs procedures at borders</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Reasons for International Trade</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Countries have different natural resources, climates, and factor endowments</li>\n  <li>Differences in technology and skill levels lead to different production efficiencies</li>\n  <li>Trade allows access to goods not available or efficiently produced domestically</li>\n  <li>Specialisation allows countries to focus on what they produce best, increasing overall efficiency</li>\n  <li>Trade can lead to lower prices for consumers through increased competition</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Theory of Comparative Cost (Comparative Advantage)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">theory of comparative advantage</span>, developed by David Ricardo, states that a country should specialise in producing goods for which it has the lowest opportunity cost, even if it is not the absolute best at producing everything. By specialising and trading, both countries can benefit, even if one country is more efficient at producing all goods than the other.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Shortcomings of the Comparative Cost Theory</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Assumes perfect factor mobility within a country, which is often unrealistic</li>\n  <li>Ignores transportation costs between trading countries</li>\n  <li>Assumes only two countries and two goods, oversimplifying real-world trade involving many countries and products</li>\n  <li>Does not account for trade barriers, such as tariffs and quotas, imposed by governments</li>\n  <li>Ignores differences in technology changes over time</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Comparative advantage is about relative (opportunity cost) efficiency, not absolute efficiency — a country can still benefit from trade even if another country is better at producing everything, as long as it specialises in its area of lowest opportunity cost.\n</div>\n",
