    "topic": "Balance of Payments (B.O.P) I",
    "topicCode": "SS3-ECO-10",
    "module": "International Economics",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">balance of payments</span> is a comprehensive record of all economic transactions between a country's residents and the rest of the world over a given period, typically one year.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Terms of Trade</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Terms of trade</span> measures the ratio of a country's export prices to its import prices. It indicates how much a country can import for a given quantity of exports.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Formula: <strong>Terms of Trade = (Index of Export Prices ÷ Index of Import Prices) × 100</strong></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Measurement of Terms of Trade</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Result</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Terms of trade > 100</td><td>\"Favourable\" — export prices have risen faster than import prices; the country can buy more imports for the same exports</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Terms of trade = 100</td><td>No change from the base year</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Terms of trade < 100</td><td>\"Unfavourable\" — export prices have fallen relative to import prices; the country must export more to buy the same quantity of imports</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Favourable vs. Unfavourable Balance of Payments</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Favourable (Surplus)</td><td>Total receipts from abroad exceed total payments made abroad</td><td>Increases foreign reserves; strengthens the national currency</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Unfavourable (Deficit)</td><td>Total payments made abroad exceed total receipts from abroad</td><td>Depletes foreign reserves; can weaken the national currency</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Effects of Balance of Payments Position</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>A persistent deficit can lead to a country borrowing heavily or depleting its foreign reserves</li>\n  <li>A persistent surplus can lead to accumulation of foreign reserves, but may also cause the domestic currency to appreciate, making exports more expensive</li>\n  <li>Balance of payments positions influence government policy on interest rates, trade restrictions, and exchange rate management</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A country relying on exporting one primary commodity (like crude oil) is especially vulnerable to unfavourable terms of trade if that commodity's global price falls, since its import capacity shrinks even if export volume stays the same.\n</div>\n",
