    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Building on the basics of the balance of payments, this topic examines the specific concepts of <span class=\"learn-keyword\">surplus and deficit</span> in more detail, along with practical methods governments use to correct balance of payments problems.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Balance of Payments Accounts Structure</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Records</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Current account</td><td>Trade in goods and services, income from investments, and transfers (e.g., remittances)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Capital/Financial account</td><td>Flows of investment capital, loans, and financial assets between countries</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Surplus and Deficit Explained</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">surplus</span> occurs when a country earns more from its international transactions than it spends; a <span class=\"learn-keyword\">deficit</span> occurs when it spends more than it earns. Persistent imbalances in either direction can create economic challenges requiring correction.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Methods of Correcting Balance of Payments Problems (Deficit)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Currency devaluation</td><td>Deliberately reducing the official value of the currency to make exports cheaper and imports more expensive</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Import restrictions</td><td>Imposing tariffs or quotas to reduce the volume of imports</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Export promotion</td><td>Providing incentives (subsidies, tax breaks) to encourage more exports</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deflationary policies</td><td>Reducing overall spending in the economy (e.g., higher interest rates) to reduce demand for imports</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Exchange controls</td><td>Government restrictions on the amount of foreign currency that can be bought or sold</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Considerations When Choosing a Correction Method</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Each method has trade-offs: devaluation can boost exports but may fuel inflation on imported goods; import restrictions can protect local industry but may invite retaliation from trading partners; deflationary policies can correct the deficit but may slow domestic economic growth and increase unemployment.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Devaluation only works to improve the balance of payments if demand for exports and imports is sufficiently price-sensitive (elastic) — WAEC sometimes tests this underlying condition (the \"Marshall-Lerner condition\") at a basic level.\n</div>\n",
