    "topic": "Economic Growth and Development",
    "topicCode": "SS3-ECO-12",
    "module": "Economic Growth and Development",
    "term": "Second Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Although often used interchangeably in everyday speech, <span class=\"learn-keyword\">economic growth</span> and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">economic development</span> are distinct concepts in economics, each with its own measures and implications.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning of Economic Growth</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Economic growth</span> refers to an increase in a country's output of goods and services over time, typically measured as the percentage increase in real Gross Domestic Product (GDP) over a period, such as a year.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Meaning of Economic Development</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Economic development</span> is a broader concept than growth. It refers to improvements in the overall well-being and quality of life of a population, including better standards of living, health, education, reduced poverty, and greater equality — not just a rise in output.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Growth vs. Development</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Economic Growth</th><th>Economic Development</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>A quantitative measure (increase in GDP)</td><td>A qualitative and quantitative measure (improved living standards)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Can occur without benefiting the wider population (e.g., if wealth stays concentrated)</td><td>Specifically implies wider improvement in people's lives</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Narrower concept, focused purely on output</td><td>Broader concept, encompassing health, education, and equality</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Indicators of Economic Development</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Indicator</th><th>What It Measures</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Human Development Index (HDI)</td><td>A composite measure combining life expectancy, education, and income per capita</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Literacy rate</td><td>The percentage of the population able to read and write</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Life expectancy</td><td>The average number of years a person is expected to live</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Infant mortality rate</td><td>The number of infant deaths per 1,000 live births, reflecting healthcare quality</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Poverty rate</td><td>The percentage of the population living below a defined poverty line</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Why the Distinction Matters</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A country can experience economic growth (rising GDP) without meaningful economic development, if that growth is concentrated among a small elite and does not translate into better health, education, or reduced poverty for the broader population — this is a common critique applied to resource-rich but unequal economies.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Growth is about \"more\" (quantity of output); development is about \"better\" (quality of life). A country can have one without the other — WAEC frequently asks candidates to distinguish the two with examples.\n</div>\n",
