    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Nigeria has adopted several national and international development frameworks over the years, each with specific goals aimed at accelerating economic growth and improving citizens' welfare.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Millennium Development Goals (MDGs)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">MDGs</span> were eight international development goals established by the United Nations in 2000, to be achieved by 2015, covering areas such as eradicating extreme poverty, achieving universal primary education, promoting gender equality, reducing child mortality, and combating diseases like HIV/AIDS and malaria. Nigeria, as a UN member, adopted these goals as part of its national development agenda.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. National Economic Empowerment and Development Strategy (NEEDS)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">NEEDS</span> was a Nigerian government economic reform programme launched in 2004, aimed at reducing poverty, creating wealth, generating employment, and reorienting values, built around four key strategies: empowering people, promoting private enterprise, changing the way government works, and strengthening implementation.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Vision 2020</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Vision 2020</span> was a long-term national development blueprint that aimed to position Nigeria as one of the top 20 economies in the world by the year 2020, through sustained economic growth, improved infrastructure, and human capital development.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Comparing the Three Frameworks</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Framework</th><th>Origin</th><th>Timeframe</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>MDGs</td><td>United Nations (global initiative)</td><td>2000–2015</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>NEEDS</td><td>Nigerian federal government</td><td>Launched 2004, medium-term</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Vision 2020</td><td>Nigerian federal government</td><td>Long-term, targeting the year 2020</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Achievements and Shortcomings</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">While these plans brought some progress — such as increased awareness of development priorities and some improvements in specific sectors — Nigeria generally fell short of fully achieving many MDG targets by 2015 and did not become a top-20 global economy by 2020, reflecting persistent challenges in implementation, funding, and political will.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: MDGs are a GLOBAL (UN) initiative that Nigeria adopted, while NEEDS and Vision 2020 are Nigeria's OWN national development programmes — WAEC often tests whether you know which framework originated where.\n</div>\n",
