    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  Over the decades, Nigeria has implemented several major <span class=\"learn-keyword\">economic reform programmes</span> aimed at restructuring the economy, addressing structural weaknesses, and promoting sustainable growth.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Structural Adjustment Programme (SAP)</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The <span class=\"learn-keyword\">SAP</span> was introduced in 1986 under President Ibrahim Babangida, at the recommendation of the IMF and World Bank, in response to Nigeria's economic crisis following the oil price collapse. Key elements included: devaluation of the naira, removal of subsidies, trade liberalisation, and privatisation of state-owned enterprises. While SAP aimed to diversify the economy and reduce government intervention, it led to significant hardship, including inflation and reduced purchasing power, for many Nigerians.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Privatisation and Commercialisation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Nigeria has pursued <span class=\"learn-keyword\">privatisation</span> (transferring ownership of state enterprises to private investors) and <span class=\"learn-keyword\">commercialisation</span> (making state enterprises operate on a profit-oriented, commercial basis while remaining government-owned) in sectors such as telecommunications, power, and banking, aiming to improve efficiency and reduce the burden on government finances.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Deregulation</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\"><span class=\"learn-keyword\">Deregulation</span> involves reducing government control and restrictions on economic activities, allowing market forces to determine prices and resource allocation — notably applied to the downstream petroleum sector in Nigeria, removing fuel subsidies to allow market-based pricing.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Objectives of Economic Reform Programmes</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Reducing government's direct role in running businesses, allowing the private sector to drive growth</li>\n  <li>Improving efficiency and competitiveness of previously state-run enterprises</li>\n  <li>Reducing fiscal burden on government from subsidising loss-making enterprises</li>\n  <li>Attracting foreign and domestic investment through a more market-friendly environment</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Criticisms of Reform Programmes</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>Reforms can increase short-term hardship for ordinary citizens (e.g., higher fuel prices after subsidy removal)</li>\n  <li>Privatisation has sometimes led to enterprises being sold to politically connected individuals at below-market value</li>\n  <li>Benefits of reform are not always evenly distributed across the population</li>\n  <li>Weak regulatory oversight can allow privatised monopolies to exploit consumers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Don't confuse \"privatisation\" (full transfer of ownership to private hands) with \"commercialisation\" (still government-owned, but run on a profit-making basis) — WAEC frequently tests this specific distinction.\n</div>\n",
